1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Welcome to Topic 5.

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:03,050
2, DHCP Server.

3
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:06,900
So far every pod address was typed by
hand.

4
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:09,950
Now a server takes over that job.

5
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:15,800
You will learn how a client gets an
address with four messages called DORA,

6
00:00:16,050 --> 00:00:17,650
and how leases renew.

7
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:23,500
You will install the DHCP Server role on
SRV01, finish its post-install wizard, and

8
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:27,750
see why the server must be authorized in
Active Directory.

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Then you build a scope with options,
reserve an address, and test it all from

10
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:35,450
CLIENT01.

11
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:38,500
DHCP stands for Dynamic Host Configuration
Protocol.

12
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:41,150
It hands out IPv4 settings automatically.

13
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:45,000
Servers like SRV01 keep static addresses,
and clients ask.

14
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:47,250
A few words to learn.

15
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:52,300
A scope is one subnet's range of addresses
the server may lease.

16
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:58,950
A lease is permission to use one address
for a set time, eight days by default.

17
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:04,800
Options are the extra settings that ride
along, like the gateway and DNS server.

18
00:01:05,050 --> 00:01:10,650
An exclusion is never handed out, and a
reservation always goes to the same

19
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:12,100
client.

20
00:01:12,350 --> 00:01:15,950
How does a client with no address get one?

21
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:17,800
Four messages, called DORA.

22
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:21,250
First, Discover: is any DHCP server out
there?

23
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:26,300
Second, Offer: the server suggests an
address, a lease time and options.

24
00:01:26,550 --> 00:01:30,150
Third, Request: the client says, I take
that offer.

25
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,000
Fourth, Acknowledge: the server confirms,
and the lease starts.

26
00:01:34,250 --> 00:01:39,050
Discover and Request are broadcasts,
because the client has no address yet.

27
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:42,500
Offer and Acknowledge can be broadcast or
unicast.

28
00:01:42,750 --> 00:01:47,950
The server listens on UDP port 67 and the
client on UDP 68.

29
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:50,600
Another subnet needs a relay agent.

30
00:01:50,850 --> 00:01:52,850
Take the default eight-day lease.

31
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:58,700
At fifty percent, day four, the client
sends a unicast request straight to SRV01

32
00:01:58,950 --> 00:02:00,150
to renew.

33
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,800
If SRV01 agrees, the clock restarts.

34
00:02:03,050 --> 00:02:08,650
If SRV01 is silent, the client keeps
trying, and at eighty-seven and a half

35
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:13,300
percent, day seven, it rebinds by
broadcasting to any DHCP server.

36
00:02:13,550 --> 00:02:19,150
If the lease runs out, the client drops
the address and starts DORA again.

37
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:25,000
With no server at all, it ends up with an
APIPA address in 169.

38
00:02:25,250 --> 00:02:26,450
254.

39
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:30,300
Installing the role looks just like
installing AD DS.

40
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:34,950
In Server Manager on SRV01, choose Manage,
Add Roles and Features.

41
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:41,200
On Server Roles, select DHCP Server, then
Add Features so you get the DHCP console

42
00:02:41,450 --> 00:02:42,650
and PowerShell module.

43
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:44,500
No restart is needed.

44
00:02:44,750 --> 00:02:49,550
When it finishes, Server Manager raises a
flag: configuration required for DHCP

45
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:53,400
Server at SRV01, with the link Complete
DHCP configuration.

46
00:02:53,650 --> 00:02:57,250
In PowerShell, one line does it:
Install-WindowsFeature DHCP, dash

47
00:02:57,500 --> 00:02:58,700
IncludeManagementTools.

48
00:02:58,950 --> 00:03:01,350
The role alone leases nothing yet.

49
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:05,200
The flag link opens the DHCP Post-Install
configuration wizard.

50
00:03:05,450 --> 00:03:11,050
Description tells you the two jobs it will
do: create the local groups DHCP

51
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:15,700
Administrators and DHCP Users, and
authorize the server in Active Directory.

52
00:03:15,950 --> 00:03:20,350
On Authorization, keep Use the following
user's credentials with SA1 backslash

53
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:21,800
Administrator.

54
00:03:22,050 --> 00:03:26,450
That account belongs to Enterprise Admins,
which is who may authorize.

55
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:27,900
Select Commit.

56
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:33,350
The Summary page should show Done for
security groups creation and Done for

57
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,200
authorizing the DHCP server.

58
00:03:35,450 --> 00:03:37,850
Close it and the flag clears.

59
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:39,700
Why does authorization exist?

60
00:03:39,950 --> 00:03:43,150
A rogue DHCP server is one nobody
approved.

61
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:49,000
Its offers could send clients to a wrong
gateway or a fake DNS server.

62
00:03:49,250 --> 00:03:55,250
In a domain, a Windows DHCP server checks
a list in Active Directory before it

63
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:56,700
leases.

64
00:03:56,950 --> 00:04:01,350
If its name is on the list, like SRV01, it
works.

65
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:03,600
If not, it leases nothing.

66
00:04:03,850 --> 00:04:07,450
The limit: only Windows DHCP servers check
that list.

67
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:13,700
A home router plugged into a wall port
does not, and switches stop those with

68
00:04:13,950 --> 00:04:15,150
DHCP snooping.

69
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:18,200
Here are the wizard's jobs in PowerShell.

70
00:04:18,450 --> 00:04:22,450
Add-DhcpServerSecurityGroup creates the
two groups; older guides use netsh dhcp

71
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:25,100
add securitygroups for the same thing.

72
00:04:25,350 --> 00:04:28,550
Then restart the DHCPServer service so
they apply.

73
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:30,800
Add-DhcpServerInDC, with the name srv01.

74
00:04:31,050 --> 00:04:32,250
sa1.

75
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:34,500
lab and the address 192.

76
00:04:34,750 --> 00:04:35,950
168.

77
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:37,400
50.

78
00:04:37,650 --> 00:04:40,450
20, puts SRV01 on the authorized list.

79
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:45,500
Get-DhcpServerInDC reads the list back,
and you should see that one line.

80
00:04:45,750 --> 00:04:50,550
Last, a Set-ItemProperty on a Server
Manager registry key tells Server Manager

81
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:54,800
the post-install work is done, so the flag
goes away.

82
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:56,250
Now the scope.

83
00:04:56,500 --> 00:04:58,900
Open the DHCP console, expand srv01.

84
00:04:59,150 --> 00:05:00,350
sa1.

85
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:03,400
lab, right-click IPv4 and choose New
Scope.

86
00:05:03,650 --> 00:05:06,850
Name it SA1 Clients, a label for people.

87
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:09,900
On IP Address Range, start at 192.

88
00:05:10,150 --> 00:05:11,350
168.

89
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:12,800
50.

90
00:05:13,050 --> 00:05:15,050
100 and end at 192.

91
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:16,500
168.

92
00:05:16,750 --> 00:05:17,950
50.

93
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:20,600
199, with a mask of 255.

94
00:05:20,850 --> 00:05:22,050
255.

95
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:23,500
255.

96
00:05:23,750 --> 00:05:24,950
0.

97
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:26,800
Our servers use .

98
00:05:27,050 --> 00:05:28,250
10 to .

99
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:31,300
50, so they stay outside the range.

100
00:05:31,550 --> 00:05:33,550
The scope ID becomes 192.

101
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:35,000
168.

102
00:05:35,250 --> 00:05:36,450
50.

103
00:05:36,700 --> 00:05:37,900
0.

104
00:05:38,150 --> 00:05:40,950
On Add Exclusions and Delay, exclude .

105
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:42,400
100 to .

106
00:05:42,650 --> 00:05:44,250
109 and select Add.

107
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:49,300
Those ten addresses are kept back for
devices you set by hand.

108
00:05:49,550 --> 00:05:50,750
The wizard continues.

109
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Lease Duration shows eight days, the
default, so keep it.

110
00:05:55,250 --> 00:05:57,650
Say yes to configuring options now.

111
00:05:57,900 --> 00:06:00,300
On Router, Default Gateway, add 192.

112
00:06:00,550 --> 00:06:01,750
168.

113
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:03,200
50.

114
00:06:03,450 --> 00:06:04,650
1.

115
00:06:04,900 --> 00:06:06,500
That is option 003.

116
00:06:06,750 --> 00:06:12,750
The pod has no router, so this is only
option data on the client side.

117
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,400
On Domain Name and DNS Servers, the parent
domain is sa1.

118
00:06:17,650 --> 00:06:21,650
lab, option 015, and the DNS server is
DC01, 192.

119
00:06:21,900 --> 00:06:23,100
168.

120
00:06:23,350 --> 00:06:24,550
50.

121
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:26,000
10, option 006.

122
00:06:26,250 --> 00:06:27,450
Skip WINS.

123
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:32,500
Finally, activate the scope now, because
only an active scope answers clients.

124
00:06:32,750 --> 00:06:35,550
The whole wizard fits in three commands.

125
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:40,600
Add-DhcpServerv4Scope takes the name,
start and end of the range, the subnet

126
00:06:40,850 --> 00:06:44,050
mask and the lease duration of eight days.

127
00:06:44,300 --> 00:06:46,300
Scopes are active by default.

128
00:06:46,550 --> 00:06:49,750
Add-DhcpServerv4ExclusionRange uses dash
ScopeId, the network address 192.

129
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:51,200
168.

130
00:06:51,450 --> 00:06:52,650
50.

131
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:58,100
0, to pick the scope, then the start and
end of the exclusion.

132
00:06:58,350 --> 00:07:02,750
Set-DhcpServerv4OptionValue sets the
router, the DNS server and the DNS domain

133
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:04,200
in one go.

134
00:07:04,450 --> 00:07:09,650
Dash DnsServer validates the DNS server
address, unless you add dash Force, so

135
00:07:09,900 --> 00:07:11,100
keep DC01 running.

136
00:07:11,350 --> 00:07:14,150
The two Get commands prove it worked.

137
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:18,000
In the DHCP console, your scope sits under
IPv4.

138
00:07:18,250 --> 00:07:23,450
Inside it, Address Pool holds the range
and exclusions, Address Leases shows who

139
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:26,900
has which address, and Reservations holds
fixed ones.

140
00:07:27,150 --> 00:07:31,550
Scope Options shows 003, 006 and 015 for
this scope only.

141
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:35,800
Further down, Server Options apply to
every scope on SRV01.

142
00:07:36,050 --> 00:07:40,050
What if the same option is set in two
places?

143
00:07:40,300 --> 00:07:42,300
The most specific one wins.

144
00:07:42,550 --> 00:07:45,350
Reservation beats scope, and scope beats
server.

145
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:50,000
The gateway differs per subnet, so it
belongs on the scope.

146
00:07:50,250 --> 00:07:53,850
A reservation always gives a client the
same address.

147
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:57,700
Under the scope, right-click Reservations
and choose New Reservation.

148
00:07:57,950 --> 00:08:01,550
Give it a name, CLIENT01, and the address
192.

149
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:03,000
168.

150
00:08:03,250 --> 00:08:04,450
50.

151
00:08:04,700 --> 00:08:05,900
150.

152
00:08:06,150 --> 00:08:09,350
It is inside the range, outside the
exclusion.

153
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:15,200
Then type the client's MAC address, which
you read as Physical Address in ipconfig

154
00:08:15,450 --> 00:08:16,650
slash all.

155
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:19,300
One wrong character means no match.

156
00:08:19,550 --> 00:08:21,950
Why not just a static address?

157
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:28,200
A reservation is managed in one place, and
the client still receives all the options.

158
00:08:28,450 --> 00:08:32,450
In PowerShell, Get-DhcpServerv4Lease lists
the active leases in the scope.

159
00:08:32,700 --> 00:08:36,300
CLIENT01's first address can be anything
free from .

160
00:08:36,550 --> 00:08:37,750
110 to .

161
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,200
199.

162
00:08:39,450 --> 00:08:41,450
Look at the ClientId column.

163
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:44,900
For Windows clients, that is the MAC
address.

164
00:08:45,150 --> 00:08:51,550
So instead of typing, you can grab it from
the lease into a variable, then run

165
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:54,600
Add-DhcpServerv4Reservation with the
scope, the address .

166
00:08:54,850 --> 00:08:57,250
150, that ClientId and the name.

167
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:01,100
After CLIENT01 releases and renews, list
the leases again.

168
00:09:01,350 --> 00:09:03,350
The address is now .

169
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:06,000
150 and the state reads ActiveReservation.

170
00:09:06,250 --> 00:09:09,850
On CLIENT01, ipconfig slash all is your
best friend.

171
00:09:10,100 --> 00:09:14,100
Physical Address is the MAC you need for a
reservation.

172
00:09:14,350 --> 00:09:15,950
DHCP Enabled says Yes.

173
00:09:16,200 --> 00:09:22,200
The IPv4 address, the gateway, the DNS
server and the DNS suffix all came from

174
00:09:22,450 --> 00:09:23,650
the scope.

175
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:27,100
DHCP Server names who gave the lease, 192.

176
00:09:27,350 --> 00:09:28,550
168.

177
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:30,000
50.

178
00:09:30,250 --> 00:09:31,450
20.

179
00:09:31,700 --> 00:09:37,700
To get a new lease, run ipconfig slash
release to give the address back, then

180
00:09:37,950 --> 00:09:40,350
slash renew to run DORA again.

181
00:09:40,600 --> 00:09:46,200
By default CLIENT01 registers its own A
record, and SRV01 would register the PTR

182
00:09:46,450 --> 00:09:48,850
record if a reverse zone existed.

183
00:09:49,100 --> 00:09:50,300
Four mistakes.

184
00:09:50,550 --> 00:09:52,950
One: skipping authorization in a domain.

185
00:09:53,200 --> 00:09:57,200
The role and scope look fine, but nothing
gets leased.

186
00:09:57,450 --> 00:09:59,450
Authorize, then check with
Get-DhcpServerInDC.

187
00:09:59,700 --> 00:10:02,500
Two: a range that covers static addresses.

188
00:10:02,750 --> 00:10:08,750
Keep servers outside the range or exclude
them, or two machines will fight over one

189
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:10,200
address.

190
00:10:10,450 --> 00:10:14,850
Three: pointing option 006 at a public or
wrong DNS server.

191
00:10:15,100 --> 00:10:18,700
Domain clients must ask a domain
controller, here 192.

192
00:10:18,950 --> 00:10:20,150
168.

193
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:21,600
50.

194
00:10:21,850 --> 00:10:23,050
10.

195
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:26,500
Four: a reservation with a mistyped MAC
address.

196
00:10:26,750 --> 00:10:29,550
Copy it carefully, then release and renew.

197
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:31,400
Time to check yourself.

198
00:10:31,650 --> 00:10:34,850
SRV01 leases from SA1 Clients for eight
days.

199
00:10:35,100 --> 00:10:41,500
First: name the four DORA messages in
order, and say why the first one is a

200
00:10:41,750 --> 00:10:42,950
broadcast.

201
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:49,200
Second: when does CLIENT01 first try to
renew its lease, and whom does it ask?

202
00:10:49,450 --> 00:10:55,050
Third: the role and scope are in place,
but no client gets an address.

203
00:10:55,300 --> 00:10:57,700
Which step was most likely skipped?

204
00:10:57,950 --> 00:11:01,950
Fourth: option 006 differs between server
options and scope options.

205
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:04,200
Which value does CLIENT01 get?

206
00:11:04,450 --> 00:11:05,650
Pause and answer.

207
00:11:05,900 --> 00:11:07,500
Here are the answers.

208
00:11:07,750 --> 00:11:09,750
One: Discover, Offer, Request,
Acknowledge.

209
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:16,000
The first is a broadcast because the
client has no address and knows no server

210
00:11:16,250 --> 00:11:17,450
yet to ask.

211
00:11:17,700 --> 00:11:23,300
Two: after four days, at fifty percent,
with a unicast request straight to SRV01.

212
00:11:23,550 --> 00:11:26,750
At seven days it broadcasts to any server.

213
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
Three: authorization in Active Directory.

214
00:11:29,250 --> 00:11:33,250
An unauthorized Windows DHCP server in a
domain leases nothing.

215
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:38,300
Four: the scope option, because the most
specific level wins: reservation first,

216
00:11:38,550 --> 00:11:40,150
then scope, then server.

217
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:41,600
Let's build it.

218
00:11:41,850 --> 00:11:48,250
On SRV01 I add the DHCP Server role, then
use the flag to run the post-install

219
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:50,900
wizard with SA1 Administrator and commit.

220
00:11:51,150 --> 00:11:53,150
The console shows green checks.

221
00:11:53,400 --> 00:11:56,200
Now a new scope: SA1 Clients, .

222
00:11:56,450 --> 00:11:57,650
100 to .

223
00:11:57,900 --> 00:11:59,100
199, excluding .

224
00:11:59,350 --> 00:12:00,550
100 to .

225
00:12:00,800 --> 00:12:04,400
109, eight days, with router, DNS server
and domain.

226
00:12:04,650 --> 00:12:09,450
On CLIENT01 I switch to automatic
addressing, and ipconfig shows a lease

227
00:12:09,700 --> 00:12:10,900
from SRV01.

228
00:12:11,150 --> 00:12:13,550
Back on SRV01 I reserve .

229
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:15,400
150 for CLIENT01's MAC.

230
00:12:15,650 --> 00:12:18,450
Release, renew, and there it is: 192.

231
00:12:18,700 --> 00:12:19,900
168.

232
00:12:20,150 --> 00:12:21,350
50.

233
00:12:21,600 --> 00:12:22,800
150.

234
00:12:23,050 --> 00:12:27,450
A client gets an address with DORA:
Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge,

235
00:12:27,700 --> 00:12:29,700
over UDP 67 and 68.

236
00:12:29,950 --> 00:12:35,950
It renews at fifty percent, rebinds at
eighty-seven and a half, and falls back to

237
00:12:36,200 --> 00:12:37,400
APIPA.

238
00:12:37,650 --> 00:12:43,250
To build a server, install the role,
create the groups and authorize it in

239
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:44,700
Active Directory.

240
00:12:44,950 --> 00:12:51,350
A scope is a range plus exclusions, a
lease time and options 003, 006 and 015.

241
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:54,400
Reservations tie an address to a MAC.

242
00:12:54,650 --> 00:12:55,850
Lab 5.

243
00:12:56,100 --> 00:12:59,300
2 practices it all in about an hour.
